2186084267 #edb/ref 1061 30811 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084267 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e4fabb97-fb83-54ac-bf48-59a6c3ed7682" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084267 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083322 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084268 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186084267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084268 #edb/ref 1061 30812 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084268 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "83423fd5-cc32-5942-8633-6f6c285f43c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084268 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186083323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084451 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that a student takes a test and gets a score of $x=82.$ If we know that the mean score for the class is $\\overline{x} = 75$ and the standard deviation is $\\sigma = 3.5,$ then how good is the student's score? \n\nTo determine how good the student's score is, we can calculate the **$z$-score**, which is defined by\n\n$$\nz = \\frac{x-\\overline{x}}{\\sigma}.\n$$\n\nThe $z$-score represents how many standard deviations the observed score is above or below the mean of the data set.\n\n- A large positive $z$-score means the student's score is high compared to the class's mean score.\n\n- A $z$-score close to $0$ means the student's score is close to the class's mean score.\n\n- A large negative $z$-score means the student's score is low compared to the class's mean score.\n\nIn this particular example, the student's $z$-score is\n\n$$\nz = \\dfrac{82 - 75}{3.5} = 2.\n$$\n\nSo, the student's score is $2$ standard deviations above the mean. This suggests that the student obtained a much higher score than the average score for the entire class." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084451 #edb/ref 1065 3697 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084451 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "79fbe8d7-9dd2-5396-a697-e06d76e71c6b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084451 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084454 #edb/ref 1064 "So far, we have practiced converting an observation $x$ into a $z$-score. Other times, though, we may be given a $z$-score and want to find the corresponding observation $x.$\n\nTo accomplish this, we can take the formula for the $z$-score and solve for $x\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z & =\\frac{x-\\overset{―}{x}}{\\sigma } \\\\ z\\cdot \\sigma  & =x-\\overset{―}{x} \\\\ x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor example, suppose that a data set has a mean of $\\overline{x}=15$ and a standard deviation of $\\sigma = 1.5.$ If we're given a $z$-score of $z=2,$ then we can find the corresponding observation $x$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\  & =15+2\\cdot 1.5 \\\\  & =15+3 \\\\  & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084454 #edb/ref 1065 3705 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084454 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "b2772228-1477-5aed-b3ec-02d21f685274" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186084454 #edb/ref 1067 "Finding an Observation Corresponding to a Given Z-Score" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302862 #edb/ref 1080 "5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302862 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186302862 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40a8a690-0e17-513a-9759-e2c32710072c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304605 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186302862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304605 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304605 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304605 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186302862 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186304605 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "c1c1b1da-a3d8-5b1d-9536-eeb4e916f1d1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305007 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186304605 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305007 #edb/ref 1069 "q-332239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305007 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305007 #edb/ref 1071 "A data set contains the observations ${x}_{1}=3.08,{x}_{2}=8.68$ with $z$-scores ${z}_{1}=-1.2$ and ${z}_{2}=2.3,$ respectively. Find the mean $\\overset{¯}{x}$ of the data set.\n\n$\\overset{¯}{x}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305007 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cbfb0339-7153-551d-aafb-c429f4a2a68d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186305007 #edb/ref 1074 "To compute the value of an observation $x$ which yields a given $z$-score in a data set with mean $\\overset{―}{x}$ and standard deviation $\\sigma ,$ we can use the following formula:\n\n$$\nx=\\overset{―}{x}+z\\cdot \\sigma \n$$\n\nFrom the first observation, we have\n\n$$\n3.08=\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nFrom the second observation, we have\n\n$$\n8.68=\\overset{¯}{x}+2.3\\cdot \\sigma .\n$$\n\nSubtracting the first equation from the second and solving for $\\sigma ,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}8.68-3.08 & =(2.3-(-1.2))\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 5.6 & =3.5\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ \\sigma  & =1.6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting this value back into our first equation and solving for $\\overset{¯}{x},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot \\sigma  \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}+(-1.2)\\cdot 1.6 \\\\ 3.08 & =\\overset{¯}{x}-1.92 \\\\ \\overset{¯}{x} & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429576 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429576 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mat